  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
